This small call shows the successful continuation path:
```javascript
function requireLabel(req, res, next) {
  if (typeof req.body?.label !== "string") return res.status(400).json({error:"Label required"});
  const clean = req.body.label.trim();
  if (!clean) return res.status(400).json({error:"Label required"});
  req.cleanLabel = clean;
  next();
}
const request = {body:{label:"  Sky  "}};
requireLabel(request, {}, () => console.log("Continue"));
console.log(request.cleanLabel);
```
This prints Continue, then Sky. trim takes no arguments and returns a string without outer whitespace. The callback passed as next takes no input here and prints Continue. In Express, the framework supplies next to continue to later middleware. Rejected input must send its response and return without calling next. The empty response object is sufficient only for this successful local demonstration, not for testing the rejection path.

This exercise uses a Map in one process. Map.has(key) checks presence; get reads the record; set stores it.

Keep the stored response separate from the object returned to a caller. Return a fresh shallow copy of the result on both a new request and a retry, so a caller changing a top-level returned field cannot overwrite the saved response. Object spread, {...savedResult}, copies top-level fields; it would still share nested objects, so this exercise uses flat result fields.

```javascript
const saved = new Map();
saved.set("req-a", {status:201});
console.log(saved.has("req-a"));
console.log(saved.get("req-a").status);
console.log(saved.has("req-b"));
console.log(saved.get("req-b"));
```
The outputs are true, 201, false, undefined. set receives a key/value, stores the entry and returns the Map; has returns presence; get returns the value or undefined. Reading a stored object does not copy it, so the response-copy rule above still matters.

This practice checks a simplified restored snapshot. Version must match, and every note ID/title pair must match.

```javascript
const rows = [{id:1}, {id:2}];
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id > 0));
console.log(rows.every(row => row.id === 1));
```
This prints true, then false. every receives a predicate called on successive entries and returns whether all tested entries pass, stopping on the first failure. An empty array returns true; it has no failing entry. It does not validate a backup version by itself.

An assertNever helper can also throw if invalid data unexpectedly reaches that branch at runtime. It does not replace validation of outside data.

Here the helper and its call appear together:
```typescript
type Signal = {kind:"on"} | {kind:"off"};
function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error("Unexpected variant");
}
function signalText(signal: Signal): string {
  switch (signal.kind) {
    case "on": return "Enabled";
    case "off": return "Disabled";
    default: return assertNever(signal);
  }
}
console.log(signalText({kind:"on"}));
```
This prints Enabled. assertNever accepts only a value narrowed to never and never returns normally. A valid handled variant never reaches that call. Adding {kind:"pause"} to Signal without adding its case makes the default call a type error: pause remains possible there. That changed program is supposed to fail compilation, not print a result.

The browser exercise represents package settings in a JavaScript object named project. Set scripts.start to node main.js and engines.node to >=22.13.0. Keep dependencies empty because no third-party runtime package is used. Object.keys(project.dependencies) gives an array of its property names; length 0 establishes there are no declared dependencies. A JavaScript object literal may use single quotes, but a real JSON file requires double quotes.

```javascript
console.log(Object.keys({}));
console.log(Object.keys({start:"node main.js"}));
```
This prints [] and ["start"]. Object.keys receives an object and returns an array of its own enumerable string-keyed property names; it does not change the object.

