The arrays are ["A", "B"] and ["A", "B", "C"]. slice receives a start index and an excluded end index, returning a new shallow array without changing matches.

The exercise has add and toggle actions. Add creates an unfinished task unless its ID already exists. Toggle changes only the matching task. An unknown action keeps the current state.

some checks whether any array entry passes a condition: tasks.some(task => task.id === wantedId) answers whether that ID is already present. To append without editing the old array, [...tasks, newTask] creates a new array containing its old entries followed by the new task.

```javascript
const tasks = [{id:"read"}, {id:"draw"}];
console.log(tasks.some(task => task.id === "read"));
console.log(tasks.some(task => task.id === "walk"));
```
This prints true, then false. some receives a predicate and returns true once an entry passes; it returns false if none pass, including an empty array. It does not change tasks.

The exercise also puts returned greetings on a page. In a browser, `document` represents the page. `document.querySelector("#g1")` finds the element with id g1; the `#` marks an id in this selector string. Assigning its `.textContent` sets its plain text. That requires the supplied HTML elements to exist. It does not treat the greeting as HTML instructions, so angle brackets in text stay text.

In a browser page containing `<p id="g1"></p>`, run:
```javascript
